window.docContents[49].resolve({"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--The--injection--and--contradiction--Tactics--Quizzes":{"contents":"Recall our RGB and Color types:\n\ninductive RGB : Type where\n  | red\n  | green\n  | blue\n\ninductive Color : Type where\n  | black\n  | white\n  | primary (p: RGB)\n\n\nSuppose Lean's proof state looks likex : RGB\ny : RGB\nh : .primary x = .primary y\n------------------------------\n⊢ y = x\nand we apply the tactic injection h with hxy.  What will happen?(1) \"No goals.\"(2) The tactic fails.(3) Hypothesis h becomes hxy : x = y.(4) None of the above.example (x y : RGB)\n    (h : Color.primary x = Color.primary y) :\n    x = y := by\n  injection h with hxy\n\n\nSuppose Lean's proof state looks likex : Bool\ny : Bool\nh : !x = !y\n--------------\n⊢ y = x\nand we apply the tactic injection h with hxy.  What will happen?(A) \"No more goals.\"(B) The tactic fails.(C) Hypothesis h becomes hxy : x = y.(D) None of the above.example (x y : Bool) (h : !x = !y) : y = x := by\n  injection h with hxy\nTactic `injection` failed: equality of constructor applications expected\n\nx y : Bool\nh : (!decide (x = !y)) = true\n⊢ y = x\n\n\nNow suppose Lean's proof state looks likex : Nat\ny : Nat\nh : x + 1 = y + 1\n-------------------\n⊢ y = x\nand we apply the tactic injection h with hxy.  What will happen?(A) \"No more goals.\"(B) The tactic fails.(C) Hypothesis h becomes hxy : x = y.(D) None of the above.example (x y : Nat) (h : x + 1 = y + 1) : y = x := by\n  injection h with hxy\n  symm\n  assumption\n\n\nFinally, suppose Lean's proof state looks likex : Nat\ny : Nat\nh : 1 + x = 1 + y\n-------------------\n⊢ y = x\nand we apply the tactic injection h with hxy.  What will happen?(A) \"No more goals.\"(B) The tactic fails.(C) Hypothesis h becomes hxy : x = y.(D) None of the above.example (x y : Nat) (h : 1 + x = 1 + y) : y = x := by\n  injection h with hxy\nTactic `injection` failed: equality of constructor applications expected\n\nx y : Nat\nh : 1 + x = 1 + y\n⊢ y = x\nThe addition in 1 + x (and 1 + y) is blocked by the variable in the second argument.\nTherefore it doesn't reduce to x.succ, so injectivity of constructors can't be used directly.\n\n\n\nThe injectivity of constructors allows us to reason that\n∀ (n m : Nat), n + 1 = m + 1 → n = m.  The converse of this\nimplication is an instance of a more general fact about both\nconstructors and functions:\n\nexample {α β : Type} (f : α → β) (x y : α)\n    (h : x = y) : f x = f y := by\n  rw [h]\n\nexample (n m : Nat) (h : n = m) :\n    n + 1 = m + 1 := by\n  rw [h]\n\n\n\n\nLean also provides congr as a tactic.\n\nexample (n m : Nat) (h : n = m) :\n    n + 1 = m + 1 := by\n  congr\n\n\n\n\nWe can specify the recursion-depth with congr n.\n\nexample (a b c d : Nat) (hab : a = b) (hcd : c = d) :\n    (a, c + 1) = (b, 1 + d) := by\n  congr\n\n\nWe now have three goals: c = 1, 1 = d, and 1 = d,\nbut these are not provable from our hypotheses! congr\nhas gone too deep.\n\nunsolved goals\ncase e_snd.e_a\na b c d : Nat\nhab : a = b\nhcd : c = d\n⊢ c = 1\n\ncase e_snd.e_a.e_2\na b c d : Nat\nhab : a = b\nhcd : c = d\n⊢ 1 = d\n\ncase e_snd.e_a.e_3\na b c d : Nat\nhab : a = b\nhcd : c = d\n⊢ 1 = d\n\n\nexample (a b c d : Nat) (hab : a = b) (hcd : c = d) :\n    (a, c + 1) = (b, 1 + d) := by\n  /- Only shallowly using `congr` here allows us to complete the proof -/\n  congr 1\n  rw [Nat.add_comm]\n  congr\n\n\nThe above proof can be made simpler by just rewriting before the congr, so arguably it doesn't require limiting the depth.example (a b c d : Nat) (hab : a = b) (hcd : c = d) :\n    (a, c + 1) = (b, 1 + d) := by\n  rw [Nat.add_comm]\n  congr\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics\u0009The  injection  and  contradiction  Tactics","header":"7.2.2. Quizzes","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--The--injection--and--contradiction--Tactics--Quizzes"}});